{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0b1fa7b234d8a13e0169bb3b97b434819191ffdc75f1b982ae", "created": 1773878167, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Online Patient Portals, MyChart, and Accessing Test Results\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS as the primary enabling infrastructure for patients to retrieve and interpret their medical information. The main functional relationship is that ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS directly support the activity of ACCESSING TEST RESULTS by letting patients view their results and related records digitally. A notable example of a portal/health record account within this community is MYCHART (HEALTH RECORD ACCOUNT), which is referenced as a type of portal used to track appointments and communicate with care teams, reinforcing the broader portal-based workflow for accessing and discussing results.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS are the hub entity enabling digital access to medical information\",\"explanation\":\"ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS are described as digital/online systems and tools that give patients access to test results and other medical information, including viewing medical records and downloading/printing them for personal retention (e.g., inclusion in a medical binder) [Data: Entities (422)].\\n\\nThe entity’s centrality in this community is also reflected in its relationships: ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS connect directly to the process of ACCESSING TEST RESULTS and to an example portal account type (MYCHART), indicating the portals serve as a bridge between patients and their health information workflows [Data: Relationships (4217, 2196); Entities (422)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Accessing test results is explicitly positioned as a patient process facilitated by portals\",\"explanation\":\"ACCESSING TEST RESULTS is defined as the patient action and overall process of retrieving medical test results to understand findings and, when needed, discuss them with the healthcare team [Data: Entities (2392)].\\n\\nONLINE PATIENT PORTALS are described as a “great way” and one available method for ACCESSING TEST RESULTS, because they allow users to view their results through an online portal, establishing a direct support relationship between the tool (portals) and the activity (accessing results) [Data: Relationships (4217); Entities (422, 2392)].\"},{\"summary\":\"MyChart is presented as an example of a portal/health record account tied to care-team communication\",\"explanation\":\"MYCHART (HEALTH RECORD ACCOUNT) is referenced as a type/example of portal/health record account used to track appointments and communicate with the care team, situating it as an instantiation of the broader ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS concept [Data: Relationships (2196)].\\n\\nThis example illustrates that portal functionality in this community is not limited to passive viewing of results; it also includes interaction and coordination features (e.g., communication) that can influence how patients follow up on and contextualize test results with clinicians [Data: Relationships (2196); Entities (422)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Portals support patient record management through viewing, saving, and sharing outputs\",\"explanation\":\"ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS are described as allowing patients to view test results and medical records, and often to download or print these materials so they can be saved or included in a personal medical binder [Data: Entities (422)].\\n\\nThis record-management functionality is directly relevant to ACCESSING TEST RESULTS, because it supports patient retention of information and the ability to bring results into other contexts (e.g., future appointments or personal organization), reinforcing the process-oriented role of accessing and reviewing findings [Data: Entities (422, 2392); Relationships (4217)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Clinical interpretation remains important because portal-displayed information can be complex\",\"explanation\":\"The description of ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS notes that the information presented can sometimes be complex, and portals may display results or details that require explanation from a clinician or care team member [Data: Entities (422)].\\n\\nThis creates an implicit workflow connection between ACCESSING TEST RESULTS (retrieving and understanding findings) and subsequent clinician discussion when needed, aligning with the definition of ACCESSING TEST RESULTS as not merely retrieval but understanding and, when appropriate, discussion with the healthcare team [Data: Entities (2392, 422)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Impact severity is low-to-moderate because the community is informational/health-workflow oriented rather than adversarial\",\"explanation\":\"The entities and relationships provided describe a patient-facing digital workflow: ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS enabling ACCESSING TEST RESULTS, and MyChart as an example portal account used to track appointments and communicate with the care team [Data: Entities (422, 2392); Relationships (4217, 2196)].\\n\\nNo data is provided indicating illegal activity, malicious technical operations, or reputational misconduct; the described functions are oriented toward access, communication, and patient understanding of medical information, which suggests operational importance but not inherently high-impact harm within the scope of the provided data [Data: Entities (422, 2392); Relationships (4217, 2196)].\"}],\"rating\":2.6,\"rating_explanation\":\"The impact severity is low because the community describes routine patient access and communication workflows (portals enabling access to results, with MyChart as an example) without any provided indicators of wrongdoing or elevated risk.\"}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens":